# Day 25

**24km / 6 hours**

The time estimate is very rough on today because there was plenty of waiting around for tides which I didn't include. That's the nature of the section of trail I'm on though. 

It began with a brutally cold morning with frost all around which made it tough to get started, although it was at least sunny. After getting going it was a pleasant enough walk past a river and on a hill area. After an hour or two I came to a town on the river and had to wait for a few hours for the tide to turn so I could kayak down the river.

![A nice stream to cross before it becomes a large river](./stream.jpg)

The kayak took around 1.5 hours although I didn't a lot of time dawdling and only worked out how to get a decent rhythm towards the end, it was a dead easy kayak though. There was basically no current except a tiny amount near the end from the wind. No rapids, no chance of capsizing or coming into trouble unless you did something strange. Was still nice though. 

![Going down the calm but murky river in a kayak](./river-kayak.jpg)

After that I truly felt like I was in Auckland, mainly because of the Aucklanders (derogatory). There was a nice but simple walk around the gardens before a walk towards the rocks. This section is heavily dependent on tide timings and the times were not on my side. I would need to either wait for hours, try to get through or just walk an alternate path along the road. In the end I waited a little bit before trying to make it along the coast. 

![The beach with its cliffs and rocks](./beach-rocks-1.jpg)

![Showing the more precarious nature of these rocks](./beach-rocks-2.jpg)

This section had some awesome cliffs and geology but because of the timings it was also pretty precarious and I was having to get somewhat wet and also do some strange stepping around on slippery rocks. Would not recommend. I did get through unscathed though and I was prepared to get wet before the waist so I was all good on that front. 

After that it was just some more beach walking but this time on a popular, highly populated beach which felt extremely uncomfortable to me since I was carrying a large pack and felt extremely out of place. 

I hate Auckland.

